{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790728358,"nonce":"BSk64uHdpTC1VIPD3nQC5PMj224=","title":"Std.downcast loses Array identity across raw and Dynamic values","message":"Issue3403 exercises Std.downcast(array, Array) before and after storing the array in Dynamic. The compiler passes a raw array pointer to haxe_std_downcast in the first case; the runtime reads it as DynamicValue and returns null. The second call passes a real Dynamic box, but the Array class token is the stable hash 382270662 while the box carries builtin type tag 7, so the runtime also returns null. After those casts work, Assert.equals still fails because it compares the boxed Dynamic array to the same raw array reference.\n\nThe compiler returns a statically known Array directly when downcasting it to Array. The runtime maps Array and String class-token hashes to their builtin box tags before checking a boxed value. Dynamic equality recognizes a raw array reference as equal to a Dynamic box holding that same array. The expected negative downcasts to G remain null.\n\nThe generic assertion helper compares its two slots through Reflect.compare with unknown type tag 0. Array boxes are recorded at creation so the raw/boxed identity check does not dereference arbitrary numeric slots. The same identity rule is applied to Dynamic equality.\n\nValidation: Issue3403 now prints CONFORMANCE_OK 10. Full conformance is 780/1094 scored on this run (146 skipped), with Issue3403 the only status change from the previous 779/1094 run; the earlier 780 high-water included a flaky pass of Issue10083. All 92 authored regressions pass, and cargo clippy and cargo fmt checks pass. Clippy still reports the pre-existing needless_bool and cloned_ref_to_slice_refs warnings.","files":null}]}